there's a creature scurrying across your  face right now yes you and at some point  maybe now maybe in a few days it's going  to find a nice cozy pore in your skin  and lay a single enormous egg meet the  face mites they're smaller than a grain  of sand are a kind of arachnid like  spiders and they feast on the oil and  cells in your skin particularly on your  oily nose cheeks and forehead  scientists suspect they've been living  on us since the dawn of humanity  over 200 thousand years ago and today  study suggests practically every adult  on the planet has thousands of them odds  are you've been living with them your  whole life babies quickly get them from  their parents a few days after birth and  once those face lights are on you the  only thing they enjoy as much a slurping  oil and nutrients from your pores is  having sex all over your face  afterwards females burrowed deep into  your pores where they lay their eggs the  eggs end up in one of two places  depending on the species a face might  the first species called demodex  folliculorum lays its eggs in your hair  follicle while the second prefers  nesting in your sebaceous glands and in  under two weeks the babies hatch meat  lay their own eggs and die leaving  behind a pile of decomposing corpses now  you can wash some of this off but you'll  never eradicate them completely because  even if you treat them with antibiotics  they'll return in about six weeks tops  you'll pick them up from towels pillows  and your loved ones well that all sounds  horrific but usually face mites are  harmless they only become a problem when  they multiply out of control this can  happen in people with an impaired immune  system it's also been seen in people  with a painful skin condition called  rosacea normally you'll have around one  or two mites per square centimeter of  skin but one study found that people  with rosacea had ten times the normal  amount believe it or not in some cases  these mites can be useful  researcher